      { speaker: "Alex", target: "Estoy de acuerdo en parte, pero quisiera matizar un punto.", translation: "I partly agree, but I’d like to qualify one point.", pronunciation: "es-TOY deh ah-KWER-doh en PAR-teh, PEH-roh kee-SYEH-rah mah-tee-SAR oon POON-toh", literal: "I am in agreement in part, but I would want to nuance one point.", why: "Alex starts with partial agreement and uses the conditional “quisiera” before the disagreement. This is academic cushioning, not hesitation." },
      { speaker: "Profesor", target: "Adelante. ¿En qué se apoya?", translation: "Go ahead. What are you basing that on?", pronunciation: "ah-deh-LAN-teh. en keh seh ah-POH-yah", literal: "Forward. On what does it support itself?", why: "The professor immediately asks for evidence. In seminar Spanish, a claim is expected to lean on a text, dataset, or passage." },
      { speaker: "Alex", target: "En los datos del capítulo tres, que no respaldan del todo esa conclusión.", translation: "On the data in chapter three, which doesn’t fully support that conclusion.", pronunciation: "en los DAH-tos del kah-PEE-too-loh tres, keh noh res-PAL-dan del TOH-doh EH-sah kon-kloo-SYON", literal: "In the data of chapter three, which do not support of the everything that conclusion.", why: "Alex names the evidence before the objection. “No respaldan del todo” limits the claim instead of declaring it wrong." },
      { speaker: "Profesor", target: "¿Podría precisar qué parte le genera dudas?", translation: "Could you specify which part raises doubts for you?", pronunciation: "poh-DREE-ah preh-see-SAR keh PAR-teh leh heh-NEH-rah DOO-das", literal: "Could you make precise what part generates doubts for you?", why: "“Podría” keeps the request formal and collaborative. The professor is asking Alex to sharpen the point, not challenging Alex personally." },
      { speaker: "Alex", target: "Claro. No me queda del todo claro cómo conecta la muestra con la afirmación general.", translation: "Of course. It is not entirely clear to me how the sample connects to the general claim.", pronunciation: "KLAH-roh. noh meh KEH-dah del TOH-doh KLAH-roh KOH-moh koh-NEK-tah lah MWES-trah kon lah ah-feer-mah-SYON heh-neh-RAL", literal: "Clear. It does not remain entirely clear to me how the sample connects with the general affirmation.", why: "“No me queda del todo claro” is a respectful academic hedge. Alex questions the reasoning without saying “that’s wrong”." },
      { speaker: "Profesor", target: "Bien. Entonces la observación sería sobre el alcance de la evidencia.", translation: "Good. Then the observation would be about the scope of the evidence.", pronunciation: "byen. en-TON-ses lah ob-ser-bah-SYON seh-REE-ah SOH-breh el al-KAN-seh deh lah eh-bee-DEN-syah", literal: "Good. Then the observation would be about the reach of the evidence.", why: "The professor reformulates Alex’s point in academic terms. “Sería” keeps the summary provisional and open to correction." },
      { speaker: "Alex", target: "Exacto; no descarto la tesis, solo quisiera limitar su alcance.", translation: "Exactly; I am not dismissing the thesis, I’d just like to limit its scope.", pronunciation: "eg-SAK-toh. noh des-KAR-toh lah TEH-sees, SOH-loh kee-SYEH-rah lee-mee-TAR soo al-KAN-seh", literal: "Exact; I do not discard the thesis, only I would want to limit its reach.", why: "Alex makes the academic move explicit: qualify, not reject. This protects the discussion from sounding adversarial." }
