      { mistake: "Using “matizar” to mean “decorate”", whyItFails: "The literal idea of shading does not mean making the argument prettier. In academic use, it means qualifying the claim.", sayInstead: "Quisiera matizar el alcance de esa conclusión." },
      { mistake: "Giving the objection before the evidence", whyItFails: "Without the textual or data basis, the comment sounds like preference. The professor will likely ask “¿en qué se apoya?”.", sayInstead: "En los datos del capítulo tres, no me queda del todo claro..." },
      { mistake: "Translating “no me queda claro” as “it does not stay clear to me”", whyItFails: "The literal translation hides the pragmatic force: it is a polite way to raise a doubt.", sayInstead: "It is not entirely clear to me..." }
