      { speaker: "Alex", target: "Buenos días. Me interesa porque llevo tres años trabajando en atención al cliente.", translation: "Good morning. It interests me because I’ve been working in customer service for three years.", pronunciation: "BWEH-nos DEE-as. meh een-teh-REH-sah por-keh YEH-boh tres AH-nyos trah-bah-HAN-doh en ah-ten-SYON al KLYEN-teh", literal: "Good days. It interests me because I carry three years working in attention to the client.", why: "“Llevo + time + gerund” is the natural Colombian structure for experience that started in the past and continues now." },
      { speaker: "Entrevistadora", target: "¿Qué tipo de casos ha manejado en ese tiempo?", translation: "What kinds of cases have you handled during that time?", pronunciation: "keh TEE-poh deh KAH-sos ah mah-neh-HAH-doh en EH-seh TYEM-poh", literal: "What type of cases have you managed in that time?", why: "“Ha manejado” keeps the focus on accumulated experience up to now, which is exactly what interviewers listen for." },
      { speaker: "Alex", target: "Por ejemplo, en mi cargo anterior atendía reclamos de facturación todos los días.", translation: "For example, in my previous role I handled billing complaints every day.", pronunciation: "por eg-SEM-ploh, en mee KAR-goh an-teh-RYOR ah-ten-DEE-ah reh-KLAH-mos deh fak-too-rah-SYON TOH-dos los DEE-as", literal: "For example, in my previous position I attended billing complaints all the days.", why: "A concrete example beats a list of qualities. “Por ejemplo” signals that the answer will be evidence, not self-praise." },
      { speaker: "Entrevistadora", target: "¿Y qué aportaría al equipo?", translation: "And what would you bring to the team?", pronunciation: "ee keh ah-por-tah-REE-ah al eh-KEE-poh", literal: "And what would you contribute to the team?", why: "The conditional “aportaría” asks Alex to connect his skill to this future team without sounding as if the job is already his." },
      { speaker: "Alex", target: "Sobre todo, capacidad de resolver problemas con la gente y mantener la calma.", translation: "Above all, the ability to solve problems with people and stay calm.", pronunciation: "SOH-breh TOH-doh, kah-pah-see-DAD deh reh-sol-VER proh-BLEH-mas kon lah HEN-teh ee man-teh-NER lah KAL-mah", literal: "Above all, capacity to resolve problems with the people and maintain the calm.", why: "This names the strength, but it is still abstract. In an interview it should be followed quickly by a specific case." },
      { speaker: "Entrevistadora", target: "¿Podría darme un ejemplo concreto?", translation: "Could you give me a concrete example?", pronunciation: "poh-DREE-ah DAR-meh oon eg-SEM-ploh kon-KREH-toh", literal: "Could you give me a concrete example?", why: "Colombian interviewers may ask directly for proof. The polite conditional keeps the question formal, but the request is very practical." },
      { speaker: "Alex", target: "Claro. Un cliente llegó molesto, revisé su caso y le propuse una solución el mismo día. Con mucho gusto le amplío.", translation: "Of course. A customer came in upset, I reviewed his case and proposed a solution the same day. I’d be very glad to expand on that.", pronunciation: "KLAH-roh. oon KLYEN-teh yeh-GOH moh-LES-toh, reh-bee-SEH soo KAH-soh ee leh proh-POO-seh OO-nah soh-loo-SYON el MEES-moh DEE-ah. kon MOO-choh GOOS-toh leh AM-plee-oh", literal: "Clear. A client arrived bothered, I reviewed his case and proposed to him a solution the same day. With much pleasure I expand it for you.", why: "“Con mucho gusto le amplío” is not excessive in Colombian professional Spanish. It frames more detail as service to the interviewer, not as bragging." }
