    note: "Colombian professional settings use “usted” by default with interviewers, and courtesy formulas like “con mucho gusto” and “a la orden” are expected rather than excessive. “Cuénteme” invites a short narrative, not a one-word answer.",
    culture: [
      { label: "Usted can be warm and professional", body: "In a Colombian interview, usted is the safest default and does not make the conversation cold. Warmth comes through tone, greeting, eye contact and service phrases such as “con mucho gusto”. Moving to tú before the interviewer does can feel too familiar because the setting has a clear evaluation role. If the interviewer later says “tutéame”, then the relationship has changed and you can mirror it." },
      { label: "Courtesy formulas are part of competence", body: "Phrases such as “con mucho gusto”, “claro que sí” and “a la orden” are not decorative in Colombian professional life. They show willingness, respect and service orientation, especially in customer-facing roles. English speakers sometimes hear them as too elaborate, but in Colombia they are often the expected smooth surface of a serious answer. Leaving them out can make an otherwise correct answer sound dry." },
      { label: "Cuénteme asks for a mini narrative", body: "When an interviewer says “Cuénteme”, the answer should have movement: context, action and result. A single phrase such as “porque me gusta” wastes the invitation. Colombian Spanish often lets the interviewer sound conversational while still expecting a structured answer. The learner’s job is to keep it short, but not empty." },
      { label: "Experience needs the right tense and proof", body: "“Llevo tres años trabajando” maps neatly onto English “I’ve been working for three years”, because both describe an activity that began before now and continues. Learners often break the mapping by using simple present in Spanish or simple past in English. The grammar alone is not enough in an interview: the statement should lead into a concrete example. That combination feels confident without sounding boastful in Colombian professional Spanish." }
    ],
